# 04 — Sprint 6: Next.js Foundation Configuration

## Status: COMPLETED

## Objective

Update the scaffolded Next.js app's root layout metadata, create the frontend environment variables file, update the shared `.env.template` with frontend variables, and replace the default landing page with a minimal redirect/page.

## Prerequisites

Read these files before starting:
- `docs/outlines/05_NEXTJS_FRONTEND.md` — directory structure, BFF pattern, SSR defaults
- `docs/outlines/06_API_GATEWAY_AUTH.md` — Gateway routes, JWT format, auth endpoints
- `docs/outlines/13_ENVIRONMENT_QUIRKS.md` — port allocation (Next.js = 3000, Gateway = 8080)
- `frontend-next/AGENTS.md` — Next.js 16 docs reference
- `AGENTS.md` — environment variable documentation rule (every `${ENV_VAR}` in config must have a `.env.template` entry)
- `frontend-next/package.json` — confirm dependency versions

## Steps

### Step 1: Create `frontend-next/.env.local`

**File to CREATE:** `frontend-next/.env.local`

This file is gitignored (per root `.gitignore` covering `.env` files). It provides local development values.

```
# API Gateway URL (used by BFF route handlers and client-side API calls)
NEXT_PUBLIC_GATEWAY_URL=http://localhost:8080

# Auth Secret (used by NextAuth or custom JWT verification in BFF)
AUTH_SECRET=dev-secret-change-in-production

# Next.js server port
PORT=3000
```

**Convention:** Use `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix only for variables that must be exposed to the browser. Server-only variables (like `AUTH_SECRET`) must NOT have the `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix — they are only accessible in Server Components and Route Handlers.

### Step 2: Update `.env.template` in repo root

**File to MODIFY:** `.env.template` (repo root)

Add these lines to the end of the file:

```
# Next.js Frontend
NEXT_PUBLIC_GATEWAY_URL=http://localhost:8080
AUTH_SECRET=change-me
```

**Why:** Per `AGENTS.md` environment variable documentation rule: every `${ENV_VAR:default}` placeholder referenced in any `application.yml` MUST have a corresponding entry in `.env.template`. This extends that rule to the frontend — any environment variable used by the Next.js app must be documented in `.env.template`.

Do NOT remove or modify any existing lines in `.env.template`. Only append the new section.

### Step 3: Update root layout metadata

**File to MODIFY:** `frontend-next/src/app/layout.tsx`

Open the file. It currently has:

```tsx
export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: "Create Next App",
  description: "Generated by create next app",
};
```

Replace the `metadata` export with:

```tsx
export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: {
    template: "%s | Insurance Management System",
    default: "Insurance Management System",
  },
  description:
    "Insurance premium estimation and policy management system",
};
```

The `template` format allows child pages to set their own title: `"Dashboard | Insurance Management System"`.

Do NOT change anything else in this file — keep the Geist font setup, the `<html>` className, and the `<body>` structure exactly as they are.

### Step 4: Replace the landing page

**File to MODIFY:** `frontend-next/src/app/page.tsx`

Replace the entire contents of this file. The current file is the default create-next-app boilerplate with Vercel/Documentation links. Replace with a minimal server component that either:

**Option A — simple redirect to dashboard (preferred):**

```tsx
import { redirect } from "next/navigation";

export default function Home() {
  redirect("/dashboard");
}
```

**Why redirect:** The root path `/` has no content of its own. Users should land on the dashboard. This is a server-side redirect — no client-side flash.

### Step 5: Verify TypeScript strict mode

**File to CHECK:** `frontend-next/tsconfig.json`

Open the file and confirm `"strict": true` is present under `compilerOptions`. It should already be there (the scaffolded app has it). If it is missing, add it.

No other changes to `tsconfig.json`.

### Step 6: Verify build

Run from repo root:

```bash
cd frontend-next && npm run build
```

This verifies that:
- The redirect in `page.tsx` compiles
- The layout with template metadata compiles
- TypeScript strict mode catches any type errors
- All existing UI components still compile

If the build fails with errors about missing `/dashboard` route, that's expected — the dashboard page doesn't exist yet (it will be created in Plan 06). For now, temporarily change `page.tsx` to return a minimal placeholder instead of `redirect("/dashboard")`, just to verify the build passes:

```tsx
export default function Home() {
  return (
    <main className="flex min-h-screen items-center justify-center">
      <h1 className="text-2xl font-semibold">Insurance Management System</h1>
    </main>
  );
}
```

Keep this placeholder and add a comment: `// TODO: replace with redirect("/dashboard") once dashboard route exists`.

### Step 7: Verify lint

Run from repo root:

```bash
cd frontend-next && npm run lint
```

Fix any lint errors before marking this plan complete.

## Acceptance Criteria

- [x] `frontend-next/.env.local` exists with `NEXT_PUBLIC_GATEWAY_URL`, `AUTH_SECRET`, and `PORT`
- [x] `.env.template` includes `NEXT_PUBLIC_GATEWAY_URL` and `AUTH_SECRET` entries
- [x] Root layout metadata shows "Insurance Management System" as default title
- [x] Root layout metadata uses template format: `"%s | Insurance Management System"`
- [x] Landing page (`page.tsx`) is either a redirect or a minimal placeholder (not create-next-app boilerplate)
- [x] `tsconfig.json` has `"strict": true`
- [x] `npm run build` completes without errors
- [x] `npm run lint` passes
